Friction push follows the short-range planned jerk, never the tracking error

What: the friction feed-forward input used by the Lateral Jerk Torque Controller (and NNLC, which is off on this
car) becomes 0.5 x (planned lateral jerk over the next actuator delay, minus a 0.1 m/s^3 deadband). The tracking
error term (stock: 1.0 x error) and the stock 1.4-2 s look-ahead filter are no longer used for friction.

Why: measured on this car (rav4-steering tools/stiction.py, 8 drives), rack friction is about 0.17 of max torque.
 - An error-driven push that large rings after bumps (2026-11-10 highway wobble with friction 0.134).
 - The stock look-ahead jerk is zero almost all the time (it needs the plan to keep one direction for 1.4-2 s),
   including at the curve unwinds where the wheel sticks and then snaps (d8 16:41, d10 13:20).
 - Simulated on d8/d10 (rav4-steering tools/sim/ljtc_sim.py): downhill curve ringing 1.4 s -> 0, peak lateral
   jerk 6.4 -> 3.9 m/s^3, highway wobble 0.062 -> 0.035; bump 3 about the same (2.6 -> 2.4 s).

Tune with the sliders: Friction 0.10 (size of the push, torque units), LAF 3.2 (measured torque per m/s^2 is 0.31).
Off switch: turn "Lateral Jerk Torque Controller" off. That gives stock v0.0 behaviour, where the friction slider
applies to the error, so set friction back to 0 at the same time.

